.TH bit-cli-peers 1  "bit-cli-peers " 
.SH NAME
bit\-cli\-peers \- Connect, sample the swarm, report peers, then exit
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbit\-cli\-peers\fR [\fB\-\-duration\fR] [\fB\-\-count\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-list\-url\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\-trackers\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-connect\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-download\-rate\fR] [\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-\-max\-upload\-rate\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-overall\-download\-rate\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-overall\-upload\-rate\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-peer\-rate\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-peers\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-peers\-total\fR] [\fB\-\-encryption\fR] [\fB\-\-transport\fR] [\fB\-\-block\-peer\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-open\-files\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-handles\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-rss\fR] [\fB\-\-seed\-ratio\fR] [\fB\-\-seed\-time\fR] [\fB\-\-stop\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-init\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-lowest\-speed\-limit\fR] [\fB\-\-peer\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-dht\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-lsd\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISOURCE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Connect, sample the swarm, report peers, then exit
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\fR \fI<DUR>\fR [default: 15s]
How long to sample the swarm
.TP
\fB\-\-count\fR \fI<N>\fR
Stop once this many distinct peers have been seen
.TP
\fB\-\-sort\fR \fI<KEY>\fR [default: addr]
Sort key, as KEY or KEY:ORDER. Keys: addr, client, speed, pieces
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
Listen port, or a range as START\-END. `0` asks the OS for a free one
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISOURCE\fR>
A .torrent path, an HTTP(S) URL, a magnet URI, an info hash, a metalink, or `\-` for stdin.

`help_heading = None` keeps it in clap\*(Aqs own "Arguments" section wherever it is flattened. Without it, a command that sets a heading before flattening this would file its own positional under that heading. `TODO/cli\-surface.md`, T\-159.
.SH TRACKERS
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Add a tracker at runtime. The .torrent is never rewritten
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
One tracker per line. A blank line separates BEP 12 tiers
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-list\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Fetch a tracker list over HTTP
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\-tracker\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Remove trackers. `*` removes all
.TP
\fB\-\-replace\-trackers\fR
Replace the torrent\*(Aqs tracker list instead of adding to it
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Tracker request timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-connect\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Tracker connect timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Override the announce interval
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR
Disable tracker announces entirely
.SH "LIMITS AND LIFECYCLE"
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-download\-rate\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Download rate cap, per torrent
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-max\-upload\-rate\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Upload rate cap, per torrent
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-overall\-download\-rate\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Download rate cap across the whole run
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-overall\-upload\-rate\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Upload rate cap across the whole run
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-peer\-rate\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Download rate cap for swarm peers, not for attached HTTP sources
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-peers\fR \fI<N>\fR
Peer connections per torrent
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-peers\-total\fR \fI<N>\fR
Peer connections across the run
.TP
\fB\-\-encryption\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: prefer]
Message stream encryption, for peer connections in both directions.

`prefer` dials with MSE and dials again in plaintext when the peer does not speak it, and accepts either. `require` refuses a plaintext peer in both directions, which is what reaches a peer configured to require encryption, at the cost of every peer that cannot do it. `off` neither offers nor accepts it.

One listening port serves both: an accepting end tells the two apart by reading the first twenty bytes, so there is no second port and nothing on the wire says which mode this run is in. `\-\-json` reports what each peer settled on as `encryption`. See `TODO/peers.md`, T\-163.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
off: Never encrypt, and refuse a peer that opens with MSE
.IP \(bu 2
prefer: Try MSE, fall back to plaintext, accept both
.IP \(bu 2
require: MSE or nothing, in both directions
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fI<MODE>\fR [default: tcp]
Which transports this run listens on and dials.

`tcp` is the default and is what every run before 2026\-08\-24 did. `utp` is BEP 29 over UDP, which carries the same peer wire protocol under LEDBAT congestion control: it yields to other traffic on the same link instead of competing with it, which is what keeps a seeding box from making its own connection unusable. `both` listens on each and lets the peer decide.

One port number serves both, because they are different protocols: `\-\-port 6881` is TCP 6881 and UDP 6881. See `TODO/bep\-coverage.md`, T\-101.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
tcp: TCP only. What every run did before this flag existed
.IP \(bu 2
utp: uTP only, BEP 29. Nothing reaches a TCP\-only peer
.IP \(bu 2
both: Listen on both and let the peer choose
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-block\-peer\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Refuse this peer for the whole run. Repeatable.

An address, an inclusive `START\-END` range, or a CIDR block, in either family. A `HOST:PORT` is refused rather than truncated: the session blocks an address, so accepting one would block every port on that host without saying so.

Checked before an incoming handshake is read and before an outgoing connection is dialled, so a blocked address never takes a connection slot. There is no state file, so this lasts for the invocation. See `TODO/peers.md`, T\-164.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-open\-files\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 128]
Payload files kept open at once.

Files open when they are first touched and the least recently opened closes when this cap is reached, so a torrent with twenty thousand files does not need twenty thousand descriptors.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-handles\fR \fI<N>\fR
Stop when the process holds more than this many handles. Off by default.

This is a backstop, not a tuning knob. A supervised deployment sets it and gets a loud exit 16 and a restart instead of a process that quietly runs the machine out of descriptors.

It was written for a specific leak, one socket per peer that connected and closed before handshaking, which `TODO/peers.md` T\-020 measured and which is fixed: six hours of `scripts/soak.ps1` see zero sockets in `CLOSE_WAIT` at every sample. A backstop for a defect that is fixed still earns its place, because the next one has not been found yet.

Counted against the whole process, so it includes threads, sockets, and payload files. Read `cost` in the report for a healthy baseline before picking a number.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-rss\fR \fI<SIZE>\fR
Stop when the process holds more than this much resident memory. Off by default.

The other backstop, for the growth beside the handles. A seeder under load grows about 0.8 MiB an hour, and TODO/memory.md T\-040 attributes most of it to the peer row librqbit keeps for every peer it has ever accepted and never reclaims: 2,907 bytes a row, measured over 2,000 of them, retained after a minute of no traffic. Nothing here frees one, so a supervised deployment sets this and gets a loud exit 16 and a restart.

Read `cost` in a healthy run\*(Aqs report for a baseline before picking a number: a seeder with nothing connected sits near 12 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\-ratio\fR \fI<RATIO>\fR
Stop seeding at this ratio. 0 means do not seed
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\-time\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Stop seeding after this long
.TP
\fB\-\-stop\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Give up if there is no progress for this long
.TP
\fB\-\-init\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR [default: 10m]
Give up if the hash check has not finished in this long.

Initialisation is reading the metadata and hash\-checking whatever is on disk, and it is where a torrent can stop making progress without failing. The error names the phase and how far the check got, which a plain deadline does not.
.TP
\fB\-\-lowest\-speed\-limit\fR \fI<RATE>\fR
Abort if the rate drops below this
.TP
\fB\-\-peer\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Try this peer before any are discovered, as HOST:PORT. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dht\fR
Disable the DHT.

With `\-\-peer` and `\-\-no\-tracker` this samples a swarm of exactly the members named on the command line and reaches nothing else.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-lsd\fR
Disable local service discovery
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH bit-cli-trackers 1  "bit-cli-trackers " 
.SH NAME
bit\-cli\-trackers \- Announce or scrape, report the result, then exit
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbit\-cli\-trackers\fR [\fB\-\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-list\-url\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\-trackers\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-connect\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-\-tracker\-interval\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-scrape\fR] [\fB\-\-scrape\-url\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-withdraw\fR] [\fB\-\-family\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISOURCE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Announce or scrape, report the result, then exit
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISOURCE\fR>
A .torrent path, an HTTP(S) URL, a magnet URI, an info hash, a metalink, or `\-` for stdin.

`help_heading = None` keeps it in clap\*(Aqs own "Arguments" section wherever it is flattened. Without it, a command that sets a heading before flattening this would file its own positional under that heading. `TODO/cli\-surface.md`, T\-159.
.SH TRACKERS
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Add a tracker at runtime. The .torrent is never rewritten
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
One tracker per line. A blank line separates BEP 12 tiers
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-list\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Fetch a tracker list over HTTP
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\-tracker\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Remove trackers. `*` removes all
.TP
\fB\-\-replace\-trackers\fR
Replace the torrent\*(Aqs tracker list instead of adding to it
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Tracker request timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-connect\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Tracker connect timeout
.TP
\fB\-\-tracker\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Override the announce interval
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR
Disable tracker announces entirely
.TP
\fB\-\-scrape\fR
Scrape instead of announcing
.TP
\fB\-\-scrape\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
The scrape endpoint, for a tracker that does not follow BEP 48.

BEP 48 derives the endpoint by replacing a trailing `announce` path component with `scrape`. A tracker whose path does not end that way has no derivable endpoint, and guessing one produces a 404 that reads like the tracker being down. This is how a caller who knows the endpoint says so. It replaces the derivation, so it names one tracker and the run has to be narrowed to that tracker.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
Port to announce, or a range as START\-END. `0` asks the OS for a free one.

The port is bound for the length of the announce, so what the tracker is told is a port something is actually listening on. The command then announces `stopped`, so the record does not outlive it.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-withdraw\fR
Announce and leave the peer record behind.

The default withdraws it with a second announce carrying `event=stopped`, because asking a tracker a question should not register a peer that is gone by the time anyone dials it.
.TP
\fB\-\-family\fR \fI<FAMILY>\fR [default: auto]
Which address family to announce over.

A tracker records the source address of the connection it was announced over, so one announce registers one of this host\*(Aqs addresses. `auto` announces once per family the tracker resolves to and reports each separately, which is what says whether both are reachable.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: One announce per family the tracker has an address in
.IP \(bu 2
v4: IPv4 only
.IP \(bu 2
v6: IPv6 only
.RE
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
