.TH bit-cli-verify 1  "bit-cli-verify " 
.SH NAME
bit\-cli\-verify \- Hash\-check existing data against the torrent
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbit\-cli\-verify\fR [\fB\-\-data\fR] [\fB\-\-per\-piece\fR] [\fB\-\-select\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\-file\fR] [\fB\-O\fR|\fB\-\-index\-out\fR] [\fB\-\-peer\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-dht\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-lsd\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR] [\fB\-\-page\-select\fR] [\fB\-\-page\-client\fR] [\fB\-\-render\fR] [\fB\-\-browser\-path\fR] [\fB\-\-browser\-port\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISOURCE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Hash\-check existing data against the torrent
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-data\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Where the payload lives. Defaults to \-\-dir
.TP
\fB\-\-per\-piece\fR
Report the result of every piece, not just the failures
.TP
\fB\-\-select\-file\fR \fI<INDEX>\fR
Verify only the files a `\-\-select\-file` download asked for. Accepts ranges: 1\-5,8.

A piece the selection does not cover is reported as not selected rather than as bad, and does not fail the run. Without this, verifying what `download \-\-select\-file` wrote reports every piece outside the selection as a failure, which is true of the bytes and wrong about the run. See `TODO/disk\-io.md`, T\-184.
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\-file\fR \fI<INDEX>\fR
Skip these files, as `\-\-select\-file`\*(Aqs complement
.TP
\fB\-O\fR, \fB\-\-index\-out\fR \fI<INDEX=PATH>\fR
Where a file was written, as INDEX=PATH, for a payload downloaded with `\-O`/`\-\-index\-out`.

`verify` looks where the bytes went rather than where the torrent said they would go, and a file the caller renamed is somewhere only the caller knows. Without this, verifying what `download \-O` wrote reports that file as missing. See `TODO/cli\-surface.md`, T\-116.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISOURCE\fR>
A .torrent path, an HTTP(S) URL, a magnet URI, an info hash, a metalink, or `\-` for stdin.

`help_heading = None` keeps it in clap\*(Aqs own "Arguments" section wherever it is flattened. Without it, a command that sets a heading before flattening this would file its own positional under that heading. `TODO/cli\-surface.md`, T\-159.
.SH "RESOLVING A MAGNET"
.TP
\fB\-\-peer\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Try this peer before any are discovered, as HOST:PORT. Repeatable.

With `\-\-no\-dht`, `\-\-no\-lsd` and `\-\-no\-tracker` this resolves the metadata from exactly the peers named here and nowhere else.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dht\fR
Disable the DHT while resolving
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-lsd\fR
Disable local service discovery while resolving
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tracker\fR
Do not announce to the magnet\*(Aqs trackers while resolving
.SH "RESOLVING A WEB PAGE"
.TP
\fB\-\-page\-select\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Take the one link on a page whose URL or text contains TEXT.

Matched case insensitively, as a substring, against both the resolved URL and the anchor text beside it. A page is still refused when this leaves more than one link, because a selector that matches two is not a selection.
.TP
\fB\-\-page\-client\fR \fI<PROFILE>\fR [default: browser]
Which client to present as when fetching a source document.

`browser` sends a current Chrome\*(Aqs header set in Chrome\*(Aqs order, which is what an origin reads before it decides which page to serve. `plain` sends `bit\-cli/<version>` and nothing else. This is the source document only: a web seed is a mirror you configured and always gets `bit\-cli`.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
browser
.IP \(bu 2
plain
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-render\fR
Read the page after its script has run, through an installed browser.

A page that builds its links in script has none of them in the HTML the server sent. This drives a Chrome or Edge that is **already installed**, over the DevTools protocol, and extracts from the DOM afterwards. It never installs a browser and never bundles one.

It needs a build with the `render` feature: `cargo build \-\-release \-\-features render`. Without one the flag is refused with a message saying so rather than silently reading the page unrendered.
.TP
\fB\-\-browser\-path\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
The browser `\-\-render` drives, when it is not where this looks.

Absent, an already\-running instance on `\-\-browser\-port` is tried, then the executables on `PATH`, then the platform\*(Aqs usual locations. A path given here is tried first and alone: naming one and getting a different browser is the tool ignoring its own instruction.
.TP
\fB\-\-browser\-port\fR \fI<HOST:PORT>\fR
Attach to a browser already listening for the DevTools protocol.

`HOST:PORT`, or just `PORT` for `127.0.0.1`. Attaching to a browser that is already up is cheaper than starting a second one.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
